# x10: 20 new guides (part 3)
try: FAQX
except NameError: FAQX={}; METAX={}
LONG.update({
'puberty-ceremony-album-design':('Puberty Ceremony Album Design Ideas',[
"Puberty ceremonies, known as manjal neerattu vizha, ritu kala samskara or half-saree functions in different regions, are major family events in South India. The album is often as important to the family as a wedding album. This guide covers page order, layout ideas, colours and practical tips for designing a respectful, beautiful ceremony album.",
"## Understand the family's priorities",
"Every family celebrates differently. Before designing, ask which rituals matter most, which relatives must appear, and whether the girl is comfortable with certain photos being included. Some families prefer an album focused on the stage function, others want the full day from preparation to the evening reception. Sensitivity matters here: the album belongs to a young person, and her comfort should guide photo selection.",
"## A practical page order",
"- **Cover:** a graceful portrait in the half saree or silk saree, with her name in a clean script.",
"- **Opening spread:** the decorated venue, the invitation and a close-up of jewellery or flowers.",
"- **Getting ready:** hair, flowers, mehendi and family helping with the saree.",
"- **Rituals:** the main ceremonies in sequence, with elders blessing her.",
"- **Family portraits:** parents, siblings, grandparents and maternal uncles, who often have a special role.",
"- **Guests and gifts:** relatives and friends on stage.",
"- **Closing spread:** a calm portrait and a short blessing or thank-you line.",
"Our [Tamil wedding album page order](/blog/tamil-wedding-album-page-order/) uses similar sequencing principles that adapt well here.",
"## Layout ideas",
"### Hero portraits",
"Give the best portraits a full page. A single strong image on one side of a 12x36 spread, balanced by three smaller ritual photos on the other, is a reliable layout.",
"### Detail strips",
"Narrow horizontal strips of four or five detail shots, such as bangles, kolam, flower garlands and the aarti plate, add richness without overwhelming the spread.",
"### Family blessing collages",
"Blessing moments repeat with many relatives. Rather than giving each a full page, group them into collages of six to nine photos with consistent spacing.",
"## Colours and backgrounds",
"Traditional palettes of turmeric yellow, maroon, green and gold suit the occasion. Pastel pinks and peach work for modern families. Pick background colours from the saree itself for a cohesive look. Golden and floral templates are a natural fit; browse the [golden album PSD tag](/tag/golden-album-psd/) and [floral album PSD tag](/tag/floral-album-psd/).",
"## Text and captions",
"Keep text minimal: her name, the date and perhaps one short line of blessing. Many families appreciate a line in Tamil, Telugu, Kannada or Malayalam; make sure your font supports the script properly, as explained in our [missing fonts guide](/blog/fix-missing-fonts-in-psd/). For caption ideas, read [wedding album captions and quotes](/blog/wedding-album-captions-and-quotes/).",
"## Size and format",
"12x36 is popular for large functions, while 12x30 or 12x18 suits smaller gatherings and gift copies for grandparents. See the [12x30 album category](/category/album-12x30/) and the [12x18 album category](/category/album-12x18/), and compare formats in [wedding album sizes in India](/blog/wedding-album-sizes-india/).",
"## Templates to start from",
"Many wedding templates adapt well to puberty ceremony albums once names and colours are changed. Start with the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) and the [south Indian wedding album PSD tag](/tag/south-indian-wedding-album-psd/), and use the [album background category](/category/album-background/) for plain pages that let portraits stand out.",
]),
'anniversary-album-design-ideas':('Anniversary Album Design Ideas for Studios',[
"Anniversary albums are a growing opportunity for studios: silver and golden jubilees, first anniversaries, and family celebrations where children surprise their parents. The best anniversary albums mix old and new photos into a story. This guide gives structure, layout and design ideas.",
"## Types of anniversary albums",
"### First anniversary",
"Usually a couple shoot plus highlights from the wedding. Light, modern and romantic designs work well.",
"### Silver (25th) and golden (50th) jubilees",
"These often include a function with family, renewed vows or a pooja, plus decades of archive photos. The album becomes a family history book.",
"### Surprise albums from children",
"Children commission an album of their parents' life together, often with old prints they have scanned. Emotional storytelling matters more than design trends here.",
"## Structuring the story",
"- **Opening:** a recent portrait of the couple and the anniversary number.",
"- **Then:** wedding photos, early years, the first home.",
"- **Growing family:** children's birth, school days, festivals.",
"- **Milestones:** travel, children's weddings, grandchildren.",
"- **Now:** the anniversary function or shoot.",
"- **Closing:** a message from the children or a simple line of thanks.",
"A clear chronology helps viewers follow decades of photos without confusion.",
"## Working with old photos",
"Old prints need care. Scan at 600 DPI or higher so they can be enlarged, and fix colour fading with a Curves adjustment on each channel. Remove dust spots with the Spot Healing Brush, but do not over-restore; a little grain keeps them authentic. Faded black-and-white photos often look best kept monochrome rather than colourised.",
"### Mixing old and new photos",
"Place old photos in smaller frames with a thin border, like prints on a page, and give recent high-resolution photos the large frames. This also hides the lower resolution of scans.",
"## Design themes",
"- **Silver jubilee:** cool greys, white and silver accents with a clean serif font.",
"- **Golden jubilee:** warm cream, gold foil textures and classic ornaments. Try the [golden album PSD tag](/tag/golden-album-psd/).",
"- **Then and now spreads:** the same pose from the wedding and today side by side. Couples love these.",
"- **Timeline pages:** a horizontal line across a 12x36 spread with photos and years along it.",
"## Text that adds meaning",
"Anniversary albums can carry more text than wedding albums. Short notes from children, years and places under photos, and a dedication on the first page all add value. Keep fonts readable for older viewers: no thin scripts for body text. Our guides on [fonts for wedding albums](/blog/fonts-for-wedding-albums/) and [album captions and quotes](/blog/wedding-album-captions-and-quotes/) help.",
"## Size and binding",
"12x36 or 12x30 suits most jubilee albums. For surprise gifts, a 12x18 book is easier to wrap and hand over. Lay-flat binding helps timeline spreads. See [how to choose album binding and lamination](/blog/album-binding-and-lamination-guide/) for options.",
"## Templates to start from",
"Use [12x36 wedding album sheets](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), [12x30 sheets](/category/album-12x30/) and plain designs from the [album background category](/category/album-background/). For the cover, try the [album cover collection](/category/album-cover/). For the overall story flow, see our [wedding album layout rules](/blog/wedding-album-layout-rules/).",
]),
'haldi-mehendi-album-pages':('Haldi and Mehendi Album Page Design Ideas',[
"Haldi and mehendi functions produce some of the most colourful and candid photos of a wedding. They deserve album pages with a different energy from the formal ceremony. This guide covers how to design haldi and mehendi spreads that feel joyful while still matching the rest of the album.",
"## Why these pages need a different approach",
"Ceremony spreads are usually symmetrical, formal and calm. Haldi and mehendi photos are full of movement, laughter, bright yellow and green, and close-up details. Using the same layout for both makes one or the other look wrong. Plan these sections as a distinct chapter with its own rhythm, but keep fonts and margins consistent with the album.",
"## Haldi spreads",
"### Colour palette",
"Turmeric yellow, marigold orange and fresh green dominate. Use backgrounds in cream, pale yellow or soft white so the photos provide the colour. A heavy yellow background with yellow-stained faces becomes overwhelming.",
"### Layout ideas",
"- **Action sequence:** three or four frames in a row showing haldi being applied, the reaction and the laughter.",
"- **Hero candid:** one large photo of the bride or groom covered in haldi, with small detail shots of the bowl, flowers and hands.",
"- **Family fun collage:** a tighter grid of relatives joining in.",
"## Mehendi spreads",
"### Showing the design",
"The mehendi pattern is the star. Include at least one sharp close-up of the hands and feet, ideally in a large frame. Clients often want to see the full design, including the hidden name of the partner.",
"### Mood and colours",
"Greens, deep oranges and jewel tones suit mehendi evenings. Floral templates work beautifully; browse the [floral album PSD tag](/tag/floral-album-psd/).",
"### Dance and music",
"Mehendi nights often include dancing. Use dynamic layouts with tilted frames or overlapping photos, but sparingly, so the page does not look cluttered.",
"## Matching photos with different lighting",
"Haldi is usually shot in daylight, mehendi often under mixed evening lighting. Correct white balance first so skin looks natural, then apply a consistent grade. See our [colour correction tips](/blog/album-colour-correction-tips/) and [colour grading guide](/blog/colour-grading-wedding-album-photos/). Yellow haldi on skin can confuse auto white balance; set it manually from a neutral white cloth or wall.",
"## How many pages to give",
"For most albums, two to four spreads for haldi and two to four for mehendi is enough. More than that and the main ceremony feels rushed. Our guide on [how many pages a wedding album needs](/blog/how-many-pages-in-a-wedding-album/) explains how to balance sections.",
"## Captions and titles",
"A small chapter title like Haldi or Mehendi Night at the start of each section helps viewers. Keep it short and use the album's main script font. For ideas, read [wedding album captions and quotes](/blog/wedding-album-captions-and-quotes/).",
"## Templates to use",
"Start with the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), plus the [Hindu wedding album PSD tag](/tag/hindu-wedding-album-psd/) and [Muslim wedding album PSD tag](/tag/muslim-wedding-album-psd/) for traditional mehendi styles. For lighter pages, use the [album background category](/category/album-background/). More ideas in our [wedding album design ideas](/blog/wedding-album-design-ideas/).",
]),
'monitor-calibration-for-album-designers':('Monitor Calibration for Album Designers',[
"If your albums print darker, warmer or more saturated than they looked on screen, the monitor is usually the cause. Calibration brings your screen close to a known standard so what you see is closer to what the lab prints. This guide explains why calibration matters, how to do it and what to do if you cannot buy a calibrator yet.",
"## Why screens and prints disagree",
"Most monitors ship far too bright, often 300 to 400 nits, with a cool blue white point. Prints are viewed by reflected light and cannot be as bright. When you edit on a bright screen, you unconsciously darken photos, and the print comes back muddy. Colour shifts add to the problem: a blue-tinted screen makes you warm up photos too much.",
"## What calibration does",
"A hardware calibrator, a small sensor placed on the screen, measures the colours your monitor actually shows and creates an ICC profile that corrects them. Your operating system and Photoshop use this profile to display colours more accurately.",
"## Target settings for print work",
"- **Brightness:** 100 to 120 cd/m² (nits). This is the single most important setting.",
"- **White point:** D65 (6500K) is the common standard. Some print workflows prefer D50, but D65 is fine for most album work.",
"- **Gamma:** 2.2.",
"Set these targets in the calibration software and follow its steps. The process takes ten to fifteen minutes.",
"## Calibrating without a device",
"Without a calibrator, you can still improve accuracy:",
"### Reduce brightness",
"Lower the monitor brightness until a white page on screen looks similar to a sheet of white paper held next to it under your normal room light.",
"### Use built-in presets",
"Many monitors have an sRGB mode. It often limits brightness and colour to a more neutral setting.",
"### Use operating system tools",
"Windows has Display Colour Calibration and macOS has a display calibrator assistant. They help with gamma and white balance by eye, though less precisely than hardware.",
"## Choosing a monitor",
"For album work, an IPS panel with close to 100 percent sRGB coverage is a sensible minimum. Wide-gamut monitors show more colours but can make sRGB files look oversaturated if colour management is not set up correctly. Matte screens reduce reflections in bright studios.",
"## Room lighting matters",
"Edit in consistent, moderate lighting. Avoid strong coloured light falling on the screen, and do not sit with a bright window behind the monitor. Check prints under daylight or a neutral LED, not a yellow tube light.",
"## Test prints close the loop",
"Even a calibrated screen does not guarantee a perfect match, because paper and lab settings vary. Print one test spread with skin tones, a dark area and a bright area, then compare it with your screen. If the print is darker, lower the monitor brightness slightly rather than brightening every photo. Our [RGB vs CMYK guide](/blog/rgb-vs-cmyk-for-albums/) covers related colour settings.",
"## How often to calibrate",
"Monitors drift over time. Recalibrate every four to six weeks for professional work, and after any change in monitor settings.",
"## Put it into practice",
"With a calibrated screen, follow our [colour grading guide](/blog/colour-grading-wedding-album-photos/) and [export settings for print](/blog/export-album-sheets-jpg-for-print/). Test on a sheet from the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), a [studio background](/category/studio-background/) portrait and a light [album background](/category/album-background/) to check how gradients and skin print.",
]),
'photoshop-slow-large-album-psd':('Fix Photoshop Running Slow with Album PSDs',[
"A finished 12x36 album sheet with a dozen high-resolution photos, textures and adjustment layers can easily pass 1 GB. When Photoshop starts lagging, freezing or showing scratch disk errors, a few settings and habits make a big difference. This guide covers practical fixes, starting with the free ones.",
"## Check memory and scratch disk settings",
"### RAM allocation",
"Open Edit > Preferences > Performance (Photoshop > Settings on Mac). Let Photoshop use 70 to 80 percent of available RAM. With 8 GB of RAM, large album files will struggle; 16 GB is a practical minimum for 12x36 work, and 32 GB is comfortable.",
"### Scratch disk",
"Photoshop uses a scratch disk when RAM runs out. In Preferences > Scratch Disks, choose your fastest drive with plenty of free space, ideally an SSD with 50 GB or more free. A full C drive is one of the most common reasons for the scratch disks are full error.",
"## Reduce history and cache",
"In Performance preferences, lower History States from 50 to around 20. Each state stores data, and big files fill memory quickly. Cache Levels of 4 to 6 suit large documents. Use Edit > Purge > All occasionally during long sessions to free memory (note that it clears undo history).",
"## Make photos the right size before placing",
"This is the biggest win. A 45-megapixel camera file placed into a small frame still carries all its pixels. Resize photos to the size they will be printed: a photo covering a third of a 12x36 sheet needs about 3600 pixels on its long side. Batch resize with File > Scripts > Image Processor, or use an action described in [Photoshop actions for album designers](/blog/photoshop-actions-for-album-designers/).",
"## Use Smart Objects wisely",
"Smart Objects protect quality but add file size. Embedded Smart Objects holding full camera files are heavy; linked Smart Objects keep the PSD smaller. Once a photo is final, you can rasterise it on a working copy to lighten the file. Read [Smart Objects for album design](/blog/smart-objects-album-design/) for the trade-offs.",
"## Simplify the layer stack",
"- **Merge finished adjustments:** if colour corrections are final, merge them into the photo on a copy.",
"- **Delete hidden layers:** unused alternatives and old versions still take memory.",
"- **Avoid many layer effects:** dozens of drop shadows and glows slow redraw.",
"- **Work in 8-bit:** 16-bit doubles memory use and is rarely needed for album sheets.",
"## Graphics processor settings",
"Enable Use Graphics Processor in Performance preferences if your GPU is supported. Update graphics drivers. If you see display glitches, try the Advanced Settings and choose Basic mode.",
"## Save smarter",
"Saving huge files takes time. In File Handling preferences, turn off Maximize PSD and PSB File Compatibility if you do not need it, which makes saving faster and files smaller. Files above 2 GB must be saved as PSB (Large Document Format).",
"## Work from a local drive",
"Editing directly from a USB drive, network folder or cloud sync folder slows everything and risks corrupting files. Copy the template and photos to a local SSD, work there, and back up afterwards.",
"## When to upgrade hardware",
"If you have applied these fixes and Photoshop still struggles, the order of upgrades that help most is: more RAM, an SSD for the system and scratch disk, then a better GPU.",
"## Related guides",
"For overall efficiency, read [how to speed up your album design workflow](/blog/speed-up-album-design-workflow/) and the [print-ready PSD checklist](/blog/print-ready-psd-checklist/). Test performance with a sheet from the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), the [12x30 category](/category/album-12x30/) or the [18x24 category](/category/album-18x24/).",
]),
})
